INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — EFN calculation using percentage-of-sales formula
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Crestview Apparel reports sales of $5,000,000 and projects $6,000,000 for the year ending December 31, 2026. Total assets ($4,000,000) vary with sales, spontaneous liabilities are $1,000,000, net profit margin is 8%, and the payout ratio is 50%. What is Crestview''s external financing needed (EFN)?', '["$360,000", "$600,000", "$560,000", "$120,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the EFN formula, EFN = (A/S)(delta-S) minus (L/S)(delta-S) minus PM x projected sales x (1 minus d). Here: 0.80($1,000,000) minus 0.20($1,000,000) minus 0.08($6,000,000)(0.50) = $800,000 minus $200,000 minus $240,000 = $360,000. Choice B ($600,000) is incorrect because it omits the retained earnings deduction, overstating external capital needs.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Exponential smoothing forecast calculation
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Whitmore Distribution uses exponential smoothing with a smoothing constant of 0.4 to forecast monthly demand. The previous month''s forecast was 500 units, but actual demand was 620 units. What is the forecast for the next month?', '["500 units", "548 units", "572 units", "620 units"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the exponential smoothing model, the new forecast = alpha(Actual) + (1 minus alpha)(Previous forecast). With alpha = 0.4: 0.4(620) + 0.6(500) = 248 + 300 = 548 units. Choice C (572) is incorrect because it reverses the weights, applying 0.6 to the actual value instead of the previous forecast, overstating the adjustment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Payables turnover and days payable outstanding
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Redstone Manufacturing reports cost of goods sold of $3,650,000 for the year ended December 31, 2025. Accounts payable at the beginning of the year were $320,000 and at year-end were $410,000. What is Redstone''s days payable outstanding (DPO)?', '["41.0 days", "32.0 days", "36.5 days", "73.0 days"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under financial analysis standards, DPO = 365 / payables turnover, where payables turnover = COGS / average accounts payable. Average payables = ($320,000 + $410,000) / 2 = $365,000. Turnover = $3,650,000 / $365,000 = 10.0. DPO = 365 / 10 = 36.5 days. Choice A (41.0 days) is incorrect because it uses only ending payables, understating turnover and overstating DPO.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Weighted moving average vs simple moving average trend interpretation
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Silverbrook Analytics tracks quarterly sales for a retail client. Sales for Q1, Q2, and Q3 of 2025 were $120,000, $140,000, and $190,000, respectively. The analyst compares a 3-quarter simple moving average forecast for Q4 with a weighted moving average assigning weights of 50% to Q3, 30% to Q2, and 20% to Q1. What does the difference between the two forecasts indicate?', '["The WMA forecast is lower because it discounts the most recent quarter", "Both forecasts produce $150,000 because they cover the same three quarters", "The SMA forecast is more responsive to Q3, producing a higher estimate than the WMA", "The WMA forecast is $11,000 higher because it emphasizes the rising Q3 sales, reflecting the upward trend"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under time-series analysis, the SMA weights all periods equally: ($120,000 + $140,000 + $190,000) / 3 = $150,000. The WMA emphasizes recent data: 0.50($190,000) + 0.30($140,000) + 0.20($120,000) = $161,000. The $11,000 difference arises because the WMA assigns more weight to the higher Q3 figure. Choice C is incorrect because the SMA, by weighting all periods equally, is less responsive to recent trends than the WMA.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Evaluate forecast vs projection for bank loan submission
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Eastbrook Industries needs to present forward-looking financial statements to First National Bank as part of a $5 million term loan application for the year ending December 31, 2026. The CFO asks the CPA whether to prepare a financial forecast or a financial projection. Management intends to present best-estimate results based on expected business conditions. What should the CPA recommend?', '["Prepare a projection, because all bank submissions require hypothetical assumptions", "Prepare a projection, because limited-use distribution restricts liability exposure", "Prepare a forecast, because it reflects expected conditions and is appropriate for general-use distribution to third parties such as lenders", "Prepare either format, because the AICPA does not distinguish between them for lending purposes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AT-C Section 305, a financial forecast presents expected results based on expected conditions and is appropriate for general-use distribution to third parties such as lenders. A projection uses hypothetical assumptions and is restricted to limited-use parties. Because Eastbrook intends to present best-estimate results, a forecast is correct. Choice B is incorrect because choosing a projection solely to limit liability misapplies the distinction, which depends on assumptions, not distribution preference.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Assess regression model and recommend multiple regression improvement
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Ashford Manufacturing uses a simple linear regression model to predict monthly utility costs (Y) based on machine hours (X). The model yields Y = $8,500 + $3.20X with R-squared = 0.62 and a standard error of $4,800. The controller notes that utility costs also depend heavily on seasonal temperature changes. What should the analyst recommend?', '["Increase the smoothing constant alpha to make the model more responsive", "Rerun the regression using a logarithmic transformation of machine hours", "Accept the current model because an R-squared above 0.50 is generally reliable", "Add an independent variable for heating or cooling degree-days to create a multiple regression model"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under regression analysis principles, R-squared of 0.62 means 38% of the variation remains unexplained. When a known omitted variable (temperature) logically influences the dependent variable, adding it as an independent variable in a multiple regression model can increase R-squared and reduce the standard error. Choice C is incorrect because an R-squared of 0.62 with a $4,800 standard error leaves significant unexplained variation that a known predictor could reduce.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Seasonal index deseasonalization and interpretation
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Harborview Retail reports actual Q4 2025 sales of $960,000. The company''s historical seasonal index for Q4 is 1.20. An analyst calculates the deseasonalized Q4 sales figure. What does the deseasonalized figure reveal about Q4 performance relative to an average quarter?', '["Deseasonalized sales are $800,000, meaning Q4 underlying performance is consistent with an $800,000 quarterly run rate", "Deseasonalized sales are $1,152,000, indicating Q4 outperformed the seasonal expectation", "Deseasonalized sales are $800,000, proving that seasonal factors had no effect on Q4", "Deseasonalized sales are $768,000, indicating Q4 underperformed after removing seasonal effects"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under time-series analysis, a seasonal index of 1.20 indicates Q4 sales are typically 20% above the quarterly average. Deseasonalized sales = actual / seasonal index = $960,000 / 1.20 = $800,000, representing the underlying level after removing seasonal effects. Choice C is incorrect because deseasonalization does not prove seasonal factors had no effect; it mathematically removes the known seasonal pattern to reveal base-level performance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Percentage-of-sales AR projection and assumption risk
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Cascade Electronics projects a 25% sales increase from $4,000,000 to $5,000,000 for the year ending December 31, 2026. The percentage-of-sales method assumes accounts receivable maintain a constant ratio to revenue. If accounts receivable were $600,000 at year-end 2025, what is the projected AR balance, and what is the key risk of this assumption?', '["$500,000; the method assumes receivables grow faster than sales", "$750,000; the method may overstate receivables if collection efficiency improves during the growth phase", "$750,000; the method produces accurate projections because historical ratios are inherently stable", "$900,000; the method automatically adjusts for credit policy changes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the percentage-of-sales method, AR/Sales = $600,000 / $4,000,000 = 15%. Projected AR = 15% x $5,000,000 = $750,000. The key risk is that historical relationships may not persist during growth. Choice C is incorrect because historical ratios are not guaranteed to remain stable since changes in credit terms, collection efficiency, or customer mix during expansion can cause actual receivables to diverge from projections.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Financial forecast vs projection definition (AICPA)
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'What distinguishes a financial forecast from a financial projection under AICPA standards?', '["A forecast uses hypothetical assumptions while a projection uses expected conditions", "A forecast may only be distributed to specified parties", "A projection reflects the entity''s best estimate of expected results", "A forecast presents expected results based on expected conditions and may be distributed for general use"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AT-C Section 305, a financial forecast presents expected results based on the entity''s expected conditions and may be distributed for general use. A projection uses hypothetical assumptions and is restricted to limited-use distribution. Choice A is incorrect because it reverses the definitions: forecasts use expected conditions, and projections use hypothetical assumptions.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: hard / L3 — Multiple regression t-statistic significance analysis
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Thornfield Manufacturing developed a multiple regression model to predict production overhead (Y) using three variables: machine hours (X1, t = 8.2), number of production setups (X2, t = 5.4), and square footage of plant space (X3, t = 1.1). The overall R-squared is 0.89. What should the analyst conclude about the model?', '["All three variables are significant because R-squared exceeds 0.85", "The model should be rejected because one variable has a low t-statistic", "Machine hours and setups are significant cost drivers, but square footage may not add meaningful explanatory power given its low t-statistic", "Square footage is the most important variable because it is applied to the largest physical quantity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under regression analysis principles, the t-statistic tests whether each coefficient differs significantly from zero. A t-statistic below approximately 2.0 suggests the variable may not be a significant predictor. X1 (t = 8.2) and X2 (t = 5.4) are clearly significant, while X3 (t = 1.1) is not. Choice A is incorrect because a high overall R-squared does not mean every individual variable is significant; some may add little incremental explanatory power.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Definition of R-squared (coefficient of determination)
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'What does the coefficient of determination (R-squared) measure in regression analysis?', '["The slope of the regression line relative to the x-axis", "The proportion of variation in the dependent variable explained by the independent variable(s)", "The average distance between observed values and the regression line", "The statistical significance of each independent variable''s coefficient"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In regression analysis, R-squared measures the proportion of variation in the dependent variable explained by the independent variable(s). An R-squared of 0.85 means 85% of the variation is explained by the model. Choice C is incorrect because it describes the standard error, not R-squared. The standard error measures the average distance between observed values and the regression line.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Exponential smoothing alpha closer to 1.0
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'In exponential smoothing, what is the effect of selecting a smoothing constant closer to 1.0?', '["The forecast becomes smoother and less responsive to recent changes", "The forecast assigns equal weight to all historical observations", "The forecast is unaffected because the constant controls only the intercept", "The forecast becomes more responsive to recent actual demand"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under the exponential smoothing formula, the smoothing constant alpha determines the weight placed on the most recent observation. A higher alpha (closer to 1.0) assigns more weight to the latest actual value, making the forecast more responsive to recent changes. A lower alpha produces a smoother, slower-reacting forecast. Choice A is incorrect because it describes the effect of a lower alpha closer to 0, not a higher one.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Evaluate CPA attestation options for prospective financial statements
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Wilshire Financial Group prepares a financial projection for a proposed $20 million hotel development, contingent on hypothetical assumptions including 85% occupancy and a $275 average daily rate. The developer asks the CPA to issue an audit opinion on the projection. Under AT-C Section 305, how should the CPA respond?', '["Accept the engagement because audit opinions can be issued on projections with clearly stated assumptions", "Accept the engagement but limit distribution to general-use parties", "Decline to issue an audit opinion because a CPA cannot audit prospective financial statements; offer an examination, agreed-upon procedures, or compilation instead", "Accept the engagement and issue a qualified opinion noting the hypothetical assumptions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AT-C Section 305, a CPA cannot issue an audit opinion on prospective financial statements because the forward-looking nature of the information precludes the evidence-gathering required for an audit. The CPA may perform an examination (positive assurance), agreed-upon procedures, or compile the projection. Choice A is incorrect because auditing standards do not extend to prospective financial information regardless of how clearly assumptions are stated.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Simple moving average forecast calculation
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Northwind Distributors uses a 4-month simple moving average to forecast demand. Actual demand for September through December 2025 was 1,200, 1,400, 1,000, and 1,600 units, respectively. What is the forecast for January 2026?', '["1,300 units", "1,333 units", "1,600 units", "1,200 units"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under time-series analysis, a simple moving average averages the most recent N periods equally. The 4-month SMA for January = (1,200 + 1,400 + 1,000 + 1,600) / 4 = 5,200 / 4 = 1,300 units. Choice B (1,333) is incorrect because it uses only three months instead of four: (1,400 + 1,000 + 1,600) / 3 = 1,333, violating the defined 4-month window.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Retention ratio change effect on sustainable growth rate
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Maplewood Industries has a return on equity of 16% and currently pays out 60% of earnings as dividends. The board is considering reducing the payout ratio to 30% to fund expansion. How does this dividend policy change affect Maplewood''s sustainable growth rate?', '["Sustainable growth decreases from 9.6% to 4.8% because lower dividends reduce investor confidence", "Sustainable growth remains at 6.4% because ROE is unchanged", "Sustainable growth increases from 9.6% to 11.2% because the retention ratio doubles", "Sustainable growth increases from 6.4% to 11.2% because retaining more earnings funds additional asset growth"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under financial analysis principles, sustainable growth rate = ROE x retention ratio. Currently: 16% x (1 minus 0.60) = 6.4%. Proposed: 16% x (1 minus 0.30) = 11.2%. Reducing dividends increases retained earnings, fueling more internal growth. Choice C is incorrect because it uses the payout ratio (60%) as the retention ratio for the current calculation, overstating the starting sustainable growth rate at 9.6%.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Debt ratio calculation
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Ironclad Security Systems reports total assets of $12,500,000 and total stockholders'' equity of $4,500,000 for the year ended December 31, 2025. What is Ironclad''s debt ratio?', '["36%", "64%", "178%", "56%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under financial analysis standards, the debt ratio = total liabilities / total assets. Total liabilities = $12,500,000 minus $4,500,000 = $8,000,000. Debt ratio = $8,000,000 / $12,500,000 = 64%. This indicates 64% of assets are financed by debt. Choice C (178%) is incorrect because it calculates the debt-to-equity ratio ($8,000,000 / $4,500,000), which measures leverage differently from the debt ratio.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Working capital definition
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'What is working capital in financial statement analysis?', '["Total assets minus total liabilities", "Cash and cash equivalents available for operations", "Current assets minus current liabilities", "Net income plus depreciation and amortization"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In financial statement analysis, working capital is defined as current assets minus current liabilities. It measures the short-term liquidity cushion available to meet near-term obligations. A positive working capital indicates the company can cover upcoming payments. Choice A is incorrect because total assets minus total liabilities measures net worth or book value, a long-term solvency metric, not short-term liquidity.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Evaluate leverage-based financing decision using solvency ratios
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Oakmont Industries projects 30% revenue growth and calculates external financing needed of $2,400,000 for the year ending December 31, 2026. The CFO considers issuing $2,400,000 in long-term bonds at 6% interest or issuing 200,000 new common shares at $12 per share. Oakmont''s current debt-to-equity ratio is 1.8 and its times interest earned ratio is 2.5. Which financing strategy is more appropriate?', '["Issue bonds because debt financing preserves existing shareholders'' equity and avoids dilution", "Issue bonds because the 6% interest rate is below the company''s cost of equity", "Issue equity because the high debt-to-equity ratio is concerning, but the TIE ratio is irrelevant to the decision", "Issue equity because current leverage ratios indicate limited debt capacity, and additional interest would further compress thin interest coverage"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under financial analysis principles, a debt-to-equity ratio of 1.8 indicates heavy leverage, and a TIE ratio of 2.5 provides only modest coverage. Issuing equity reduces leverage and avoids additional interest expense that could push TIE toward dangerous levels. Choice A is incorrect because, while debt avoids dilution, the company''s already elevated leverage and thin interest coverage indicate limited remaining debt capacity, making equity the more prudent choice.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: easy / L2 — Return on assets calculation
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Bayfield Corp. reports net income of $120,000, average total assets of $1,000,000, and average stockholders'' equity of $400,000 for the year ended December 31, 2025. What is Bayfield''s return on assets?', '["12.0%", "30.0%", "8.3%", "40.0%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under financial analysis standards, return on assets = net income / average total assets = $120,000 / $1,000,000 = 12.0%. ROA measures how efficiently assets generate profit. Choice B (30.0%) is incorrect because it divides net income by average equity ($120,000 / $400,000), which calculates return on equity (ROE), not ROA.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Leading economic indicator identification
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Which of the following is classified as a leading economic indicator?', '["Building permits", "Real GDP", "Unemployment rate", "Consumer price index"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under economic analysis frameworks, leading indicators change direction before the overall economy shifts, providing early warning signals. Building permits are a leading indicator because new construction activity precedes economic expansion or contraction. Choice C (unemployment rate) is incorrect because it is a lagging indicator that typically rises after a recession has begun and falls after recovery is underway.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — Simple linear regression prediction
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Glendale Manufacturing uses a regression model to estimate monthly overhead: Y = $25,000 + $8.50X, where X is direct labor hours. If the company plans 6,000 direct labor hours next month, what is the estimated overhead?', '["$51,000", "$76,000", "$25,000", "$150,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under regression analysis, the predicted value is calculated by substituting the planned activity level into the equation: Y = $25,000 + $8.50(6,000) = $25,000 + $51,000 = $76,000. The $25,000 intercept represents fixed overhead, and $8.50 per hour is the variable rate. Choice A ($51,000) is incorrect because it includes only the variable component, omitting the $25,000 fixed overhead.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Degree of operating leverage (DOL) calculation
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Falcon Aerospace reports revenue of $3,000,000, contribution margin of $1,800,000, and EBIT of $600,000 for the year ended December 31, 2025. What is Falcon''s degree of operating leverage (DOL)?', '["0.33", "5.0", "3.0", "1.67"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under the DOL formula, DOL = contribution margin / EBIT = $1,800,000 / $600,000 = 3.0. This means a 1% change in sales produces a 3% change in EBIT, reflecting the firm''s fixed cost structure. Choice B (5.0) is incorrect because it divides revenue by EBIT ($3,000,000 / $600,000), which is not the DOL formula. DOL specifically uses contribution margin, not revenue.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Higher alpha effect on persistent forecast underestimation
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Clearwater Logistics uses exponential smoothing with alpha = 0.2 to forecast weekly shipments. Demand has shifted upward due to a new contract, and the forecast consistently underestimates actual volumes. An analyst proposes increasing alpha to 0.7. What is the most likely effect of this change?', '["Forecasts will become smoother, reducing the gap between forecast and actual demand gradually", "Forecasts will adjust more quickly to the new demand level, reducing the persistent underestimation bias", "Forecasts will not change because alpha affects only the variance, not the mean of predictions", "Forecasts will overreact to random noise equally as much as they improve tracking of demand"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under exponential smoothing principles, increasing alpha from 0.2 to 0.7 assigns 70% weight to the most recent actual value instead of 20%. When demand has shifted permanently upward and forecasts consistently underestimate, a higher alpha enables faster convergence to the new level. Choice D is incorrect because while higher alpha increases sensitivity to noise, the primary benefit in a sustained demand shift is eliminating the persistent forecast bias.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Sensitivity analysis definition
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'What does sensitivity analysis measure in prospective financial analysis?', '["The impact of changing one input variable at a time on a projected outcome", "The probability that a forecasted result will fall within a confidence interval", "The combined effect of changing all input variables simultaneously", "The historical accuracy of prior forecasts compared to actual results"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under prospective analysis techniques, sensitivity analysis measures the impact of changing one input variable at a time on a projected outcome. This helps identify which assumptions drive the most risk. Choice C is incorrect because it describes scenario analysis, which changes multiple variables simultaneously to model different states of the world such as best-case, base-case, and worst-case outcomes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — EFN as plug figure in pro forma balance sheet
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Brookfield Enterprises uses the percentage-of-sales method to prepare pro forma financial statements. After projecting 20% sales growth, the analyst calculates that projected total assets exceed the sum of projected spontaneous liabilities and existing equity plus retained earnings by $380,000. What does this $380,000 represent?', '["A surplus that should be returned to shareholders as a special dividend", "The external financing needed, which must be raised through new debt or equity to support projected growth", "A forecasting error requiring the analyst to reduce the sales growth assumption", "The amount by which projected depreciation exceeds capital expenditures"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the percentage-of-sales method for pro forma financial statements, the difference between projected total assets and the sum of projected liabilities plus equity (including retained earnings) represents external financing needed (EFN). This $380,000 gap must be filled through new debt or equity issuance. Choice C is incorrect because the gap is not a forecasting error but the natural output of the model identifying additional capital requirements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L2 — Horizontal analysis percentage change calculation
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Greyfield Corp. reports total revenue of $800,000 in 2024 and $920,000 in 2025. Using horizontal analysis, what is the percentage change in revenue?', '["13.0%", "15.0%", "87.0%", "115.0%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under horizontal analysis, percentage change = (current year minus base year) / base year x 100. Here: ($920,000 minus $800,000) / $800,000 x 100 = 15.0%. The base year (2024) serves as the denominator. Choice A (13.0%) is incorrect because it divides the change by the current year amount ($120,000 / $920,000), using the wrong reference point.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Percentage-of-sales inventory projection
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Stratton Manufacturing uses the percentage-of-sales method to project its pro forma balance sheet. Current revenue is $4,000,000 and projected revenue for the year ending December 31, 2026, is $5,000,000. Inventory is $600,000, representing 15% of current revenue. What is the projected inventory balance?', '["$600,000", "$690,000", "$750,000", "$150,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under the percentage-of-sales method, balance sheet items that vary proportionally with sales maintain a constant ratio. Inventory ratio = $600,000 / $4,000,000 = 15%. Projected inventory = 15% x $5,000,000 = $750,000. Choice B ($690,000) is incorrect because it applies a 15% growth rate to current inventory ($600,000 x 1.15), confusing the inventory-to-sales ratio with a percentage increase.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Evaluate projection limited-use distribution restriction
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Ridgeway Capital prepares both a financial forecast and a financial projection for a proposed real estate venture. The forecast is based on expected occupancy rates, and the projection models a scenario where a major tenant vacates. The developer wants to distribute both documents to potential investors. Under AT-C Section 305, what should the CPA advise?', '["Compile both documents for general distribution because compilation provides no assurance", "Compile the forecast for general use and examine the projection for general use", "Examine both documents because compilation is not permitted for forecasts", "Compile the projection for limited-use distribution only, and offer examination or compilation for the forecast, because projections cannot be distributed for general use"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AT-C Section 305, projections are restricted to limited-use distribution because they are based on hypothetical assumptions. Only the forecast, based on expected occupancy rates, can be distributed for general use to investors. The projection cannot be given to general-use recipients regardless of engagement type. Choice A is incorrect because compilation status does not override the distribution restriction; projections remain limited-use.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Adjusted R-squared vs R-squared for model comparison
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'An analyst at Pinnacle Analytics builds two regression models to predict quarterly sales. Model A uses two independent variables and has R-squared = 0.82. Model B uses five independent variables and has R-squared = 0.84. Both use the same 40-quarter data set. What should the analyst consider before selecting Model B?', '["Model B is clearly superior because its R-squared is higher by 2 percentage points", "Model B is inferior because using more variables always produces overfitting", "The analyst should compare adjusted R-squared values, because adding variables always increases R-squared even if the new variables lack predictive power", "Both models are unreliable because R-squared must exceed 0.90 to be valid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under regression analysis principles, adding independent variables to a model always increases R-squared or keeps it the same, even if the new variables have no genuine predictive value. Adjusted R-squared penalizes the addition of unhelpful variables, making it the appropriate metric for comparing models with different numbers of predictors. Choice A is incorrect because a higher R-squared alone does not indicate a better model when additional variables are involved.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Standard error interpretation and prediction reliability
(4, 'Financial Statement Analysis', 'Pembrook Consulting builds two regression models to predict annual operating costs using machine hours. Model A has a standard error of $12,000 and R-squared of 0.90. Model B has a standard error of $35,000 and R-squared of 0.91. Both use the same data set. What does the difference in standard errors indicate?', '["Model A produces more reliable predictions because its lower standard error means observed values cluster more tightly around the regression line", "Model B is more reliable because its higher R-squared demonstrates stronger explanatory power", "Both models are equally reliable because their R-squared values differ by only 1 percentage point", "Neither model is reliable because standard errors exceeding $10,000 indicate poor fit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under regression analysis, the standard error measures the average distance between observed values and the regression line. A lower standard error indicates tighter clustering and more reliable point predictions. Despite Model B''s slightly higher R-squared (0.91 vs. 0.90), its standard error of $35,000 is nearly three times Model A''s $12,000, indicating wider prediction intervals. Choice B is incorrect because a 1-point R-squared advantage does not offset a substantially larger standard error.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
